    label((0.6, 0.4), [the compiled GPU code], col: accent)
    node(0.7, 1.3, 6.5, 1.1, text(size: 13pt)[general-purpose kernels], col: dim)
    node(0.7, 2.7, 6.5, 1.1, text(size: 13pt)[`shade_pixel`, `finish_pixel`])
    node(0.7, 4.1, 6.5, 1.1, text(size: 13pt)[*+* a small "draw" entry], col: glow)
    arrow((7.2, 4.65), (10, 4.65))
    node(10, 4.0, 5, 1.3, [translated (naga)], col: dim)
    arrow((15, 4.65), (17.8, 4.65))
    node(17.8, 4.0, 5, 1.3, [older GPU language])
    arrow((22.8, 4.65), (25, 4.65))
    node(25, 4.0, 2.8, 1.3, [WebGL], col: accent)
    arrow((7.2, 1.85), (25, 1.85), col: dim)
    node(25, 1.2, 2.8, 1.3, [WebGPU], col: accent)
  }))
  WebGPU (the modern browser GPU interface) runs general-purpose kernels; the older WebGL can only colour pixels. The per-pixel work is in two functions, so each build also gets a small entry point that just colours a pixel with them, translated for WebGL. #note[Spells, collisions, anti-aliasing and music need the general kernels, so the fallback does without.]
]

